chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-38-<section class="happened">
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-39-<h2>What happened</h2>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:40:<p>The second stage was built, and it was built as a matchmaker. Dor Yeshorim has run since 1983: young people in Orthodox Jewish communities are tested and given an anonymous number, and when a match is proposed the two numbers are checked together, before the couple meets, returning one answer — proceed, or incompatible. It has screened hundreds of thousands of people across eleven countries. <!-- CHECK: dor-yeshorim --> That is Lem's p. 345 device, operating on the branch he called "a correct decision": serious recessive disease. It is not operating on the other two. Nobody is screened out for a low predicted intelligence or an unstable personality, and the programme is bounded to communities that asked for it.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:41:<p>Outside those communities the second stage went somewhere else entirely — not to the matchmaker but one step down the reproductive pipeline, to the embryo. Companies sell polygenic scoring of IVF embryos; in June 2025 one launched a product ranking them on IQ, height, hair and eye colour, anxiety and ADHD alongside disease risk, from about $5,999. <!-- CHECK: nucleus-embryo-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: embryo-screening-2024 --> The trait list overlaps his and extends past it. This is a different device from the one he described, applied to a different object, and this essay counts it as a third path rather than as his second stage arriving late.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:42:<p>The first stage — the psychosomatic matchmaker becoming general — is harder to score than it looks. Meeting online overtook meeting through friends around 2013 and is now the most common way US heterosexual couples meet, <!-- CHECK: online-dating-2019 --> but that is a finding about where people meet, not evidence that a compatibility algorithm chose for them. What certainly did not arrive is the second half of his sentence. No mass platform discourages a marriage or claims the authority to; none has become the norm against which a match could be a forbidden fruit. Dor Yeshorim is the exception that shows the condition: it acquired that authority because the community already had it, and Lem never named that as a requirement.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-43-<p>The premise underneath was never established either way. Lem rests his forecast on the machine's "better knowledge" and flags his own evidence as limited. The best-known test since is narrower than it is usually reported: machine learning over more than a hundred self-reported traits could modestly predict how much a person would desire partners in general, and could not predict how two particular people would feel before meeting. <!-- CHECK: joel-2017 --> That is a result about initial attraction, not about whether matched marriages last, and the question he actually asked is still unanswered.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-44-<p>The loud path ran the course his sentence about disgust predicts. He Jiankui announced CCR5-edited twins in November 2018; a third edited child was born the following year, confirmed by the court that convicted him in December 2019 and sentenced him to three years, with a lifetime ban from reproductive medicine. <!-- CHECK: he-jiankui-2018 --> In 2025 the major cell and gene therapy societies called for a ten-year moratorium on heritable editing — a professional call, not a ban. <!-- CHECK: germline-moratorium-2025 --> Cutting up bodies drew a prison sentence. Comparing two ID numbers over the telephone has drawn nothing.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:45:<p>His deflations held. No population's average life expectancy is near a hundred; gains have decelerated since 1990 and radical extension is judged implausible this century without slowing aging itself. <!-- CHECK: olshansky-2024 --> No astronaut has had a digestive system removed; what exists is the "microattachments" clause — implanted intrathecal pumps, subdermal hormone implants. <!-- CHECK: implanted-pumps --> And on difficulty he was wrong in the direction he could not have checked: distributing high intelligence is not "relatively easy" by any known route. Selection among a couple's own embryos is bounded by the variance that segregates within one family — about 2.5 IQ points for the top of ten on current predictors, with wide intervals, though that figure rises as scores improve. <!-- CHECK: karavani-2019 --> Whether assortative matching over many generations could do more is exactly what his machine was for, and nobody has run it.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-46-</section>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-47-
--
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-52-<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Object level</strong></td></tr>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-53-<tr><td>Second-level selection: the matchmaker matches "genotypes with other genotypes", rejecting pairings likely to produce offspring "disabled (which will be a correct decision)" (p. 345)</td><td>Built, and built as a matchmaker: since 1983 two prospective partners' anonymous test numbers are compared before they meet, returning proceed or incompatible, for serious recessive disease <!-- CHECK: dor-yeshorim --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:54:<tr><td>The same selector applied to the other two classes he names — "a low level of intelligence, or with unstable personalities (which raises some doubts, at least for the time being)" (p. 345)</td><td>Not by that device. Nobody is screened out of a marriage on predicted intelligence; the trait menu appears instead in polygenic scoring of IVF embryos, a different mechanism on a different object <!-- CHECK: nucleus-embryo-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: embryo-screening-2024 --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-55-<tr><td>Machine-mediated pairing has "a significant chance… to be widely adopted" (p. 345)</td><td>Machine-mediated meeting is now the commonest route to a US heterosexual couple, though the receipt establishes where people met, not that an algorithm matched them <!-- CHECK: online-dating-2019 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-56-<tr><td>"When it becomes a cultural norm, the marriage discouraged by the 'machinic matchmaker' will perhaps be a forbidden and thus tempting fruit" (p. 345)</td><td>No mass platform discourages a marriage or claims standing to. Where the norm did arrive, it was carried by a community that already held that authority — a precondition he never names <!-- CHECK: dor-yeshorim --> <!-- CHECK: online-dating-2019 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:57:<tr><td>"It would be relatively easy… to aim to distribute widely high intelligence as a natural characteristic of the species" (p. 346)</td><td>Untested by his route and hard by every other. Selection among one couple's embryos is capped by within-family variance — about 2.5 IQ points for the top of ten on current predictors, rising with predictor quality <!-- CHECK: karavani-2019 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-58-<tr><td>"'Marriage machines' could take thousands of years… solely capable of maximizing evolution's natural speed" (p. 347)</td><td>Not settled. No population-level shift has been demonstrated by any route, and a per-family choice made in one generation is not a species-level rate <!-- CHECK: karavani-2019 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-59-<tr><td>Cyborgization rejected: no removal of the digestive system, only "microattachments" delivering substances (pp. 342–343)</td><td>Implanted intrathecal pumps and subdermal hormone implants are routine; no space programme has proposed removing organs <!-- CHECK: implanted-pumps --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
--
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-71-
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-72-<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:73:<p>He assumed the machine would acquire authority by being right. The forecast is that once "better knowledge" is demonstrated the technology becomes "a cultural norm", after which a discouraged marriage is a forbidden fruit. Neither half happened where the machines actually went. The better knowledge was never demonstrated — the evidence he cites he calls limited, and it has not improved. And the platforms that matched hundreds of millions of people acquired no standing to discourage anyone; they rank and they queue, and a user who ignores them has broken no rule. The one place the norm exists, it was not earned by the machine at all: Dor Yeshorim works because a community that already had authority over marriage chose to route it through a test. That precondition is the load-bearing part, and it is the part he leaves out.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:74:<p>He also assumed the unit of selection would stay the couple, which is why the trait branch went somewhere he was not looking. In vitro fertilisation moved the decision past conception, and the menu he wrote — intelligence, personality — is now sold as embryo scoring rather than partner screening. That is a third path, not his second stage arriving late, and this essay scores it as one. Whether it can do anything at population scale is unsettled; whether his own route could have done more, over the millennia he allowed it, nobody has tested and probably nobody will.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-75-
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-76-<section class="open">
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-77-<h2>Still open</h2>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-78-<ul>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:79:<li><strong>Whether any of it moves a population.</strong> Neither the partner screening nor the embryo scoring has been shown to shift a trait distribution in a birth cohort. What would close it: a measured shift attributable to either — or a demonstration that within-family variance and uptake cap both below detection.</li>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-80-<li><strong>Whether the consent problem has a general answer.</strong> His statement of it (pp. 345–346) has not been improved on. One bounded community answered it by already being able to decide together. What would close it: a mechanism by which a population authorises a change to its own genetic composition, rather than individual purchases aggregating into one.</li>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-81-<li><strong>Whether the two paths converge.</strong> Lem asserts they reach "analogous results" (p. 347); this essay does not score that, because nothing tests it. Editing writes variants no parent carries; selection can only rearrange what they have. What would close it: heritable editing at a scale that outruns selection, or a demonstration that the achievable ends are the same.</li>
--
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-86-<h2>Reading</h2>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-87-<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "The Autoevolutionary Machine" (pp. 344–347) is the chapter, and it opens by telling you which images to ignore; "Cyborgization" (pp. 341–344) is the proposal he demolishes first. Read note 2 (pp. 368–369) before the staged debate at pp. 297–302 — it is where he argues with himself instead of with a constructed opponent, and it is better.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:88:<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> three sources carry the argument. Dor Yeshorim's own description of its process, which is Lem's device in operation; Karavani et al., "Screening Human Embryos for Polygenic Traits Has Limited Utility" (<em>Cell</em>, 2019), for what selection can and cannot buy; and the reporting on Nucleus Embryo (2025) for what is now on sale. The rest is in the claims register.</p>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-89-</div>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html-90-
--
chapters/07-information-farming.html-24-<p class="kicker">Essay 7 · Lem ch. 7, "The Creation of Worlds" (2013 tr., pp. 237–293)</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-25-<h1>The two sieves</h1>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:26:<p class="lede">Halfway through this chapter Lem stops forecasting and files a design, one of the most detailed in the book: a machine to produce scientific theories without a scientist — not by symbol-pushing, which he thinks cannot represent what needs representing, but by <em>growing</em> them in vats of synthetic polymers. The vats do not exist, and the automation arrived from inside the formalism he was escaping. The more interesting failure is elsewhere. In his own endnote he describes a second selector the chapter never mentions — wired not to the phenomenon but to whoever wants the answer — and stakes the proposal on it. That is where his aim is worst and his diagnosis best, in the same three pages.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-27-
chapters/07-information-farming.html-28-<section class="said">
--
chapters/07-information-farming.html-30-<p>The chapter opens on a civilisation with a good engine and no rudder: science wins its game with Nature every time and is dragged along by the winnings, so that "instead of developing a strategy, it ends up just practicing tactics" (p. 237). The remedy is an automated science, and he begins where automation stops. "Many cyberneticists are currently working on the possibility of automating hypothesis formation" (p. 239); the wall they hit is combinatorial, six variables needing "thirty thousand such machines… over a period of several dozen years" (p. 239) — though only for variables "at least for us, i.e., as long as we cannot see any link between them" (p. 239), an escape clause that does a great deal of work.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-31-<p>Then the specification: "We are to invent a device that will gather information, generalize it in the same way the scientist does, and present the results of this inquiry to experts. The device collects facts, generalizes them, and checks the validity of this generalization by applying it to a new set of facts" (p. 243). And immediately: "Our device thus produces theories" (p. 243) — theories in the strict sense he then spells out, structural equivalents of a phenomenon whose transformations track its trajectory.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:32:<p>The design follows. A fertilised egg computes an organism without writing anything down: "Could we not take up 'information farming,' cross-breed bits of information with one another, and initiate their 'growth' so that we eventually obtain a 'mature organism' in the form of scientific theory?" (pp. 251–252). The carriers are "large molecules of synthetic polymers" (p. 263) whose development is <em>isodynamic</em> with the phenomenon studied — not a picture of it, a process running alongside it. They pass through sieves, each "connected (e.g., by means of automatic manipulators, perceptrons, etc.) to a real phenomenon" (p. 264) whose actual state is encoded as molecular specks and crashed against the carriers. Those that predicted it survive; repeat, and you climb from "a theory of the examined class" toward "a theory of the class of classes" (p. 265). The law installed in place of biological fitness is the crux: "that which expresses the environment most adequately survives" (p. 259).</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-33-<p>He states Ashby's position fairly first — "new theories count for nothing… while the only things that really matter are elimination and selection" (pp. 260–261) — and does not dispute it; what he rejects is the <em>source</em> of the variants: "Ashby thinks that one should start from the greatest diversity and then 'filter' it gradually. We, in turn, want to start from significant yet not enormous diversity" (p. 261). He is explicit that formal generation and empirical selection already work as a pair: "Mathematicians are thus a generator of diversity, whereas empiricists are the selector postulated by Ashby" (p. 261). His bet against formalism is a bet about <em>representation</em>: write fetal development out in chemical notation and the formulae run "in the realm of quadrillions. There would not be enough surface across all the oceans and the entire mainland for it" (p. 251). Hence the choice between mathematics "encoded in the binary elements of large electron machines" and "the kind that actualizes the fertilized egg without any formalism whatsoever" (p. 251), and hence the rule at p. 263: "We need to represent processes with some other processes, not with formal signs."</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-34-<p>And then there is note 8 — three pages of small type at the back of the book (pp. 365–367), and it takes most of the design apart. A farm, he writes, "can actually exacerbate, rather than diminish, a crisis resulting from the excess of information" (p. 365), because it "only offers alternative ways of proceeding; it reveals what can be achieved but not whether we should proceed at all", and "Such a decision-making process must not be mechanized" (p. 365). Worse, one running too far ahead of the civilisation that started it produces information that "would not only be useless but also incomprehensible," at which point "the criteria for eliminating irrelevant information would disappear" and it becomes "a 'bomb'—if not a megabyte then at least a gigabyte one" (p. 366). The condition on the apparatus is exact: "As long as the selectors at the information farm are under the active influence of intelligent beings, they are capable of selecting information effectively" (p. 366). So there are two selectors in Lem: one wired to the phenomenon, deciding what is true, one wired to the addressee, deciding what is worth saying.</p>
--
chapters/07-information-farming.html-40-<h2>What happened</h2>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-41-<p>Parts of the p. 243 device got built, well short of the sentence. In 2009 a robot called Adam proposed functions for orphan genes in yeast, designed the experiments, ran them on its own liquid handlers and confirmed them. <!-- CHECK: adam-2009 --> In 2023 Coscientist, a language-model system, planned and executed palladium-catalysed cross-couplings on a robotic platform. <!-- CHECK: coscientist-2023 --> In 2026 <em>Nature</em> published a human-authored report on an end-to-end system; three of its papers entered blind review at a machine-learning workshop, one cleared the bar and was withdrawn under a protocol agreed in advance. <!-- CHECK: ai-scientist-2026 --> Gather, generalise, test, hand to experts: the list is ticked. But none of it produces theories in the sense he defines two sentences later — Adam found gene functions, not a theory of gene function.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:42:<p>None of it is his farm. There are no polymer carriers, no isodynamic development, no vats. Every system here represents processes with formal signs — executable ones, which is the move he did not consider: not a written score but a formalism that runs. FunSearch is the cleanest case: a language model proposes programs, an evaluator runs and scores them, survivors are fed back as prompts. <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --> That is not his sieve — its evaluator computes a formal criterion exactly, it is not "a real phenomenon" (p. 264) reporting its own state — but it is not off his map either. It is the pairing he named at p. 261, mathematicians generating and empiricists selecting, with both halves mechanised.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-43-<p>Where a selector is really coupled to the phenomenon, his rule holds: Adam's hypotheses lived or died on yeast growth curves, Coscientist's routes in the flask. Where a proxy stands between, results move. Berkeley's autonomous lab reported 41 novel compounds from 58 targets in seventeen days unattended, phase identification automated from diffraction data; <!-- CHECK: alab-2023 --> crystallographers reanalysed all of it and argued that no new materials had been made. <!-- CHECK: alab-critique-2024 --> In January 2026 the authors corrected the paper: 36 compounds from 57 targets, "novel" dropped from the title, novelty redefined as new to the prediction platform rather than to science, 36 of 40 claimed successes confirmed on manual re-analysis. <!-- CHECK: alab-correction-2026 --> That is not the failure the critique described, and it is more useful to Lem than a failure would be: the selector was right about what it was wired to, and wrong about what nobody had wired it to check. The same shape appears in a graph network's 380,000 predicted-stable crystals, in a database that by 2025 had duplicates and calls for correction. <!-- CHECK: gnome-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: gnome-duplicates-2025 --></p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-44-<p>On the second sieve the record splits, and not as the chapter expects. The deluge arrived — from ordinary human production, with no farm anywhere near it. Publication doubles every nine years; <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --> more than 10,000 papers were retracted in 2023, a record, mostly from compromised review and paper mills; <!-- CHECK: retractions-2023 --> and a 2025 analysis found the brokerages selling authorship in mass-produced papers large, resilient and growing faster than the legitimate literature — his "works that are only produced to obtain an academic degree or to satisfy someone's ambition", industrialised. <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --> He expected that pressure to strengthen selection at the gate; in the venues these papers reach, it did not. Meanwhile the addressing technology he ruled out was built first and worked: Garfield's Science Citation Index appeared in 1964, the year of this book, indexing 613 journals by counting citations — the "frequency analysis" he called worthless. <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> In 1998 PageRank extended citation analysis to hyperlinks, citing Garfield. <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --> What was never built is the machine he specified: one "sending the right kinds of information to the right kinds of people" (p. 367).</p>
--
chapters/07-information-farming.html-52-<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Object level</strong></td></tr>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-53-<tr><td>"A device that will gather information, generalize it in the same way the scientist does, and present the results of this inquiry to experts… Our device thus produces theories" (p. 243)</td><td>Closed gather–hypothesise–test loops exist: Adam (2009), Coscientist (2023), an end-to-end system through blind workshop review (2026). None produces a theory of a class <!-- CHECK: adam-2009 --> <!-- CHECK: coscientist-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: ai-scientist-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:54:<tr><td>Formal signs cannot carry it — a written score for embryogenesis would need formulae "in the realm of quadrillions" (p. 251) — so "We need to represent processes with some other processes, not with formal signs" (p. 263), in polymer carriers developing isodynamically (pp. 263–265)</td><td>The representation problem is real and the carriers were never built; what carried it was a formalism that <em>runs</em> — executable state, not written signs — neither of the two options he posed <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: adam-2009 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-55-<tr><td>Without conditions: "a still remote state when it will be easier to control phenomena than to understand all the conditions that enable such control" (p. 276)</td><td>AlphaFold2 predicts structure without the folding physics; a learned model beats the leading operational weather ensemble on 97.2% of targets without solving its equations <!-- CHECK: alphafold-nobel-2024 --> <!-- CHECK: gencast-2024 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-56-<tr><td>Conditional on crossbreeding machine algorithms with "nonalgorithmic streams of information emerging from phenomena supervised by self-organizing gradients": "causal language will stop being comprehending" (p. 277); and a causal language "of the next kind" crossing "the threshold of 'understanding'" (p. 280)</td><td>The crossbreed has not occurred and science has not stopped explaining. Where text now materialises its content — designed protein sequences that fold as specified — that is his <em>first</em> causal language <!-- CHECK: rfdiffusion-2023 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
--
chapters/07-information-farming.html-58-<tr><td>"It seems that the crisis of information distribution will lead to the strengthening of publication criteria… so that their original selection prevents the flooding of the professional market with insignificant works" (p. 367)</td><td>Not in the venues the flood reaches: a record 10,000+ retractions in 2023, brokered paper mills outgrowing the legitimate literature. The hedge was warranted; the dilemma is live <!-- CHECK: retractions-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-59-<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:60:<tr><td>The truth sieve as a design rule: "that which expresses the environment most adequately survives" (p. 259), the selector taking its verdict from the phenomenon itself</td><td>Holds where obeyed — Adam's assays, Coscientist's reactions. Where a proxy stands in, results move: the A-Lab correction, the duplicate structures <!-- CHECK: alab-correction-2026 --> <!-- CHECK: gnome-duplicates-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-61-<tr><td>The addressing condition: selectors work "as long as they are under the active influence of intelligent beings"; detached, the output is "not only useless but also incomprehensible" and drowns its creators (p. 366)</td><td>Confirmed from a direction he was not modelling: no farm exists, and the deluge came from ordinary human production doubling every nine years <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --> <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-62-<tr><td>A farm "reveals what can be achieved but not whether we should proceed at all", and "Such a decision-making process must not be mechanized" (p. 365)</td><td>Untested: systems now propose research directions, but none has been handed the choice of which to pursue <!-- CHECK: ai-scientist-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
--
chapters/07-information-farming.html-85-<h2>Reading</h2>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-86-<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "Information Farming" (pp. 239–266) is the design; p. 243 the specification, p. 251 the bet against formalism, pp. 263–265 the apparatus. Read note 8 (pp. 365–367) first: it is where he grades his own proposal, and it is better than the proposal. "Linguistic Engineering" (pp. 267–281) holds two separate forecasts, pp. 276–277 and p. 280.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:87:<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> three sources carry the argument. Szymanski et al. on the A-Lab (<em>Nature</em>, 2023), the Leeman et al. reanalysis (<em>PRX Energy</em>, 2024) and the author correction (<em>Nature</em>, Jan 2026) are the proxy-selector story in one chain; Garfield, "'Science Citation Index' — A New Dimension in Indexing" (<em>Science</em>, 1964) is the thing Lem was looking at when he called it worthless; Richardson et al. (<em>PNAS</em>, 2025) is the deluge. The rest are in the claims register.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-88-</div>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-89-

