chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:31:<p>The first chapter had already supplied the failure modes, and essay 1 grades them. What matters here is a quieter admission Zylinska records: "I do not see myself as subjectively infallible" (p. 18), and her judgement that Lem "is arguably more interested in signaling certain problems and posing questions about them than in offering any determined visions of either the present or the future" (p. 18). That sentence is an objection to this whole book. If he was posing problems rather than predicting, then scoring him against 2026 is a category error dressed as rigour.</p>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:71:<p>Not a chapter, a habit, and essay 1 named it: he reached for the successor that abolished the most. The air-cushion vehicle rather than a different motor in the same wheels; polymer carriers rather than a formalism that runs; partner selection rather than a decision moved past conception. His own second chapter contains the antidote — evolution changes what is cheap to change — and he did not turn it on his own forecasts. Where he was wrong, he was usually wrong in the direction of the more impressive machine.</p>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:79:<li><strong>Whether naming a failure mode helps anyone.</strong> Essay 1's finding is that it did not help Lem: he listed three modes and was caught by two, four paragraphs after listing them. This book listed his error in its own rules and was caught by it four times. What would close it: any evidence that forecasters who study failure taxonomies score better than those who do not.</li>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:85:<p><strong>Lem:</strong> the Conclusion (pp. 351–352) is his own defence of the book's method and takes five minutes; chapter 1 (pp. 24–30) is the failure taxonomy, and essay 1 grades it. Zylinska's introduction is worth reading for pp. 20–21, on the four editions and on what Lem's later self-assessments were for.</p>
