+**BLOCK — do not ship: the essay breaches its chapter contract, manufactures both of its negative grades, puts problem-level generalisations in the object ledger, and relies on receipts and cross-essay summaries that the book has already withdrawn or contradicted.**
+
+# Consolidated review: Essay 1
+
+The mechanical gate passes (`389` registered quotations, `116` claim markers, no broken links), but that result is not probative here: Essay 1 has only 17 quote rows although its HTML contains many more quotations, and several claims are attached to receipts that establish something narrower.
+
+Corpus checks below use `scripts/find.py -p 24` through `-p 30` and p. 355. Chapter 1's five endnotes are translator's notes; there is no missed later-Lem self-grade in this edition.
+
+## Confirmed findings, ranked
+
+### 1. Critical — The essay does not perform the job assigned by `CONTEXT.md`
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 1 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+The operative contract asks for Lem's case for forecasting and problem-extrapolation against Tetlock, prediction markets, AI timelines, and EA/x-risk, ending with the question whether futurology became his proposed discipline or the gadget astrology he mocked. The essay instead declares the other essays' ledgers “the one dataset qualified to test” a three-part error taxonomy. None of the required 2026 forecasting culture appears, and §8 records the chosen pitch without amending §6.
+
+The omitted corpus material is not incidental. Pages 24–25 define the book's subject and method: technologies are means toward intended and unintended goals; their mechanisms interest him less than the splitting or replacement of goals. Either the taxonomy becomes one section inside a contract-compliant rebuild, or the contract must be explicitly amended before redrafting.
+
+### 2. Critical — The fusion `MISS` is manufactured
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 2; Agy 1 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+Page 28 says, “Current estimates situate the production of the microfusion cell in the 1990s, or even later.” Three things prevent the current `MISS`:
+
+1. “Current estimates” reports a consensus; it is not an own-voice Lem forecast. The very next sentence says the timing of the particular technology is not the issue.
+2. “Or even later” supplies no terminal bound. The essay silently turns an unbounded hedge into a 1990s deadline.
+3. `MISS` requires no live path. The essay itself cites a live experimental programme. ITER's 2024 baseline targets research operations in 2034 and the start of deuterium-tritium operations in 2039; it does not call 2039 “fully operational,” and ITER is not a grid power plant. [ITER baseline summary](https://www.iter.org/sites/default/files/media/2024-07/baseline_press_conference_summary_july-2024_b.pdf)
+
+Remove the object row. At most, the sentence can support prose about Lem repeating a reported consensus after describing reverse errors. `OPEN` is safer than the reviewers' suggested `EARLY`, because the estimate was not his and “microfusion cell” is not simply ITER.
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH:** `fusion-spending` establishes budgets and construction cost only. It does not establish “Fusion did not produce industrial power,” “No fusion power on any grid,” or the 2039 schedule. Those present-day claims need their own receipt or must be cut.
+
+### 3. Critical — The completeness `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER` grades a claim Lem expressly disclaimed
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 3 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+Before enumerating anything, Lem says there are “an infinite variety of reasons” and that he will list “several such mistakes that are particularly displeasing to the artist” (p. 28). He neither calls the list exhaustive nor offers it as all reasons prediction fails. The essay turns three literary irritants into a complete taxonomy, invents a fourth category, and debits him for omitting it. Its own Still open section then concedes the proposed fourth mode may be a straight-line case.
+
+Delete the completeness row. “Impressive successor” may remain as the essay's tentative analytic label, but it cannot pay a verdict against Lem.
+
+### 4. Critical — The taxonomy rows mix layers and inflate generic observations into `HIT`s
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 4 and 11; Agy 2 — **CONFIRMED**, with one qualification.
+
+Sudden turns, straight-line extrapolation, and closed narrative schemas are claims about forecasting failure: problem level. The ledger places them under object level and pays them with object claims imported from other chapters. The p. 25 maxim about unintended consequences is likewise a problem-level generalisation paid by modern analogies, not a specific 1964 object forecast.
+
+The reviewers overstate one point: testing a forecaster's diagnostic on his later forecasts is not logically circular by itself. The actual defects are sufficient without that claim: these rows do not pair one specific Lem claim with one specific 2026 fact, several imported facts are wrong, and the placement violates the mandatory layer separation.
+
+Move any defensible taxonomy tests to problem level, narrow each to an independently evidenced case, and do not award three separate `HIT`s merely because later examples can be narrated in the taxonomy's vocabulary.
+
+### 5. High — Three imported examples contradict the current book
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 5–7 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+- **SETI cost:** Essay 3 now calls Lem's cost comparison `OPEN`: the $10M/year programme borrowed public dishes, and nobody assembled the like-for-like all-in figure. Essay 1 resurrects the withdrawn “wrong by two orders of magnitude” claim. `breakthrough-listen-2015` establishes only the $100M ten-year programme.
+- **Heredity:** Essay 8 scores the partner-genotype selector itself `HIT` (Dor Yeshorim since 1983) and embryo scoring as a different, third path. “His machine screens partners, and what arrived screens embryos” suppresses the device that did arrive. “Neither was available … on his desk” is false: chapter 8 discusses genotype selection, bottled fetuses, and the reproductive pipeline.
+- **Executable formalism:** Essay 7 says Lem rejected written formal signs as adequate representations, while already accepting generate-and-select at p. 261. What arrived was a formalism that runs, a third option he did not consider. “Betting explicitly against the formalism that produced it” restores the interpretation Essay 7 was revised to remove.
+
+Language-at-scale may be described as an omission, as `CONTEXT.md` requires; an omission is not a failed forecast and cannot by itself turn the sudden-turn row into a specific `HIT`.
+
+### 6. High — The sick-man passage is quoted and interpreted selectively
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 8 — **CONFIRMED**, except that the reviewer overstates the effect of the sequel.
+
+The quoted sentence is entirely on p. 29, not pp. 29–30, and begins “To a certain degree.” The essay omits that qualifier without an ellipsis. Lem immediately calls the comparison “rather shortsighted” and adds the new costs brought by a cure. The essay also invents “the one he thinks nobody notices”; the corpus says no such thing.
+
+The sequel does not erase the closed-schema point, as the reviewer implies; it complicates it by adding the surprising price of remedy. Restore the qualifier, correct the page, include the sequel, and distinguish Lem's 1964 observation about the already-persistent nuclear standoff from the essay's later VR and SETI analogies.
+
+### 7. High — The paragraph count and chronology are false
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 9 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+Blackett, Thirring, the “reverse errors,” the 1955 fusion optimism, and the microfusion estimate form one paragraph spanning pp. 27–28. “Four paragraphs after mocking Blackett” is false. More importantly, the fusion sentence occurs before Lem enumerates the three mistakes, so Essay 0's claim that he was caught “four paragraphs after listing them” reverses the printed order.
+
+Use “in the same paragraph, as the reverse-error contrast” if the example survives at all. Correct the lede, What happened, Reading, `CONTEXT.md`'s status entry, and Essay 0.
+
+### 8. High — Present-day claims outrun their receipts
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 10; Agy 4 — **CONFIRMED**, with the following item-level adjudication.
+
+| Essay claim | Status | Corpus/register check |
+|---|---|---|
+| ITER “fully operational” in 2039 | **CONFIRMED** | `fusion-spending` has no schedule. ITER says start of DT operations, not full operation or electricity production. |
+| Waste “not permanently disposed of anywhere at scale” | **CONFIRMED** | It has no marker and “at scale” hides the relevant edge case. As of 4 Aug 2026, industrial-scale Onkalo still awaited the government operating licence and aimed for readiness by year-end, so actual spent-fuel disposal had not begun. The reviewers' suggested “with rare exceptions” would itself be false. Say exactly that and receipt it. [Posiva status](https://www.posiva.fi/en/index/news/pressreleasesstockexchangereleases/2026/stuk8217ssafetyassessmenttakestheworld8217sfirstfinaldisposalfacilityclosertoanoperatinglicence.html) |
+| Hovercraft “retreated to a single scheduled route” | **CONFIRMED** | The register says the Southsea–Ryde service is “one of very few,” not the sole route. |
+| EVs “displaced” combustion | **CONFIRMED** | `ev-share-2025` says 25% of new-car sales; “is displacing” is supportable, completed displacement is not. |
+| “Impressive machine of 1966” | **CONFIRMED** | No receipt supports 1966, and the 1974-edition caveat makes the date especially misleading. |
+| A-Lab was “wrong about what nobody had wired it to check” | **CONFIRMED** | The correction establishes 36/40 confirmations, four inconclusive, and a narrowed meaning of novelty. It does not establish that the platform tested and got an unwired property wrong; “did not establish novelty beyond the platform” is supportable. |
+| “This book found it three times” | **CONFIRMED** | The prose names only two cases and supplies no third count. |
+| VR was promised as “successor-to-reality or a fad” | **CONFIRMED** | `idc-vr-peak` is shipment evidence, not evidence that these were the two promises; Essay 6 makes no such grade. |
+| Neither sudden turn was available on Lem's desk | **CONFIRMED** | False for heredity; unreceipted as intellectual history for language-scale training. |
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH:** the sentence that the translation follows an altered 1974 edition is attached to `baseline-1964-ch1`, whose sources establish Blackett, Thirring, and Bellamy but not the edition history. Use the book's `summa-editions` receipt or add the edition source.
+
+### 9. High — Most Lem quotations are outside the quote gate
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 13 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+The HTML contains many quotation spans absent from the 17 Essay 1 rows. The reviewer's examples on pp. 27–29 match the corpus. The row for “Then he will make use of it” is filed at p. 15, where Zylinska repeats the line in her introduction; the essay quotes Lem's chapter text on p. 26. Thus the gate passes against the wrong occurrence while the intended occurrence is unregistered.
+
+Regenerate every Essay 1 quote row from the final HTML. File the sick-man excerpt on p. 29 and the “Then” sentence on p. 26.
+
+### 10. Medium — Removing the manufactured negatives leaves the required negative unresolved
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 14 — **CONFIRMED as a diagnosis; proposed replacement verdicts are UNVERIFIABLE**.
+
+Once the fusion `MISS` and completeness `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER` are removed, the essay no longer meets the pre-ship requirement for an honest negative. The contract question is the right place to look, but the essay contains no Tetlock/markets/timelines evidence from which to award the reviewer's proposed `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER`.
+
+The p. 26 claim that technology can be dealt with only by another technology is also not cleanly refuted by the existence of arms-control agreements: verification, deterrence, and enforcement themselves use technologies. Its current receipts—an interpretability argument and a professional call for a germline moratorium—do not settle the universal claim. Research it, leave it `OPEN`, or state honestly that this chapter supplies no negative.
+
+### 11. Medium — Essay 0 already contradicts the correction this review requires
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH**.
+
+Essay 0 first identifies “partner selection over millennia” versus embryo selection as a manufactured negative (line 40), then later repeats the same framing as Lem's characteristic failure: “partner selection rather than a decision moved past conception” (line 71). Current Essay 8 says both routes arrived in bounded forms and explicitly refuses to count embryo screening as Lem's second stage arriving late. The downstream synthesis therefore contradicts both itself and its source essay.
+
+When Essay 1 is rebuilt, recalculate Essay 0's census and revise its “Where he was worst” and Still open sections from the new rows rather than preserving the current conclusion.
+
+### 12. Low — The lede invents “two working futurists”
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH**.
+
+The corpus names Blackett as a professional forecaster, Bellamy as a utopian novelist, Thirring as the author of a book Lem had read, unnamed “prophets of astronautics,” and a reported 1955 consensus. It does not show Lem grading “two working futurists.” Name the two and support the category, or change the sentence to the narrower fact the chapter contains.
+
